<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saverne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Zabern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saverne (German Zabern) is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where the road from Strasbourg crosses the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. After 1262 it was the main residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Strasbourg, whose great Château des Rohan was rebuilt there from 1779. In May 1525 Duke Antoine of Lorraine destroyed a peasant army near the town during the German Peasants' War, and in 1913 incidents with the German garrison caused the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne-affair">Zabern Affair</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saverne.jpg" alt="Saverne" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Krahl (Rotkraut) · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateau_Rohan_Saverne_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Saverne is a commune in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bas-rhin">Bas-Rhin</a> department of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, 45 kilometres north-west of Strasbourg. It lies on the river Zorn at the foot of a pass over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges Mountains</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a>–Rhine Canal and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>–Strasbourg railway cross it. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Tres Tabernae Caesaris, Caesar's three taverns, from the three inns that once stood on the way to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> plateau, where oxen were changed because of the steep climb.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saverne was an important place in Roman times and, after being destroyed by the Alemanni, was rebuilt by the emperor Julian. After the settlement of the Alemanni it became part of the Germanosphere, and after 870 it belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, which became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. In 1525, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, insurgents occupied the town and were driven out by Duke Anton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>. It suffered heavily in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and its episcopal palace, destroyed then, was later rebuilt. The French annexed the town after 1680.</p><p>In 1852 Louis Napoleon converted the episcopal palace into a residence for widows of knights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> of Honour. Saverne was conquered by Imperial Germany after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1913 it was the scene of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saverne-affair">Saverne Affair</a>, which gave rise to the term Zabernism, from the German name Zabern, meaning abuse of military authority or unwarranted aggression.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town's emblem is a unicorn. Legend says a unicorn's horn was found in one of the ruined castles nearby, though it was more likely a narwhal's tooth. Its principal building is the Château des Rohan, the former residence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Strasbourg, rebuilt by Cardinal de Rohan in 1779 and used by the Germans as barracks; it now houses the town museum with its Roman and Celtic collection. Other sights include the 15th-century Château vieux, the parish church of Notre-Dame-de-la-Nativité, a former Franciscan monastery with a cloister decorated with 17th-century frescoes, and the decorated Maison Katz. The ruined castles of Haut-Barr, Grand Geroldseck, Ochsenstein and Greifenstein lie nearby.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saverne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1525 Duke Antoine destroyed a peasant army of the German Peasants’ War near <b>Saverne</b> in Alsace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Lorraine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Strasbourg became in effect a self-governing free imperial city, and the bishops moved their seat to <b>Saverne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-8">8 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1262 the citizens defeated Bishop Walter of Geroldseck at Hausbergen and the city became the Free Imperial City of Strasbourg, so the bishops governed from <b>Saverne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-strasbourg">Prince-Bishopric of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
